  - invalid birth_month, "month is required" (V01): snap 400 "Each person needs a birth month" -- accurate.
  - birth after start (V04 new row / V05 existing Christine / V06 earlier start 1970-01): snap 400 "Future Kid's birth month (2030-01) is after the plan's start date (2026-09)",
    "Christine's birth month (2027-01) is after ... (2026-09)", "Christine's birth month (1971-08) is after ... (1970-01)" -- accurate.
  - duplicate id (V07), name required (V08), no primary (V09), two primaries (V10), two spouses (V11): 400 "Invalid person data: persons: <validator text>" -- accurate.
  - healthcare_persons not found (V18 linked removal; V17 blank id on the linked primary): pre-empted, 400 "Remove Christine's / Darrell Gallion's healthcare entry first".
  - at least one person / id required / invalid role: pre-empted by parsePersonsForm (V15, V16 IDENTICAL to base; blank ids are auto-generated).
  DEFECT -- invalid birth_month, "invalid month %q" sub-shape (V02 "1990-13", V03 "19711-08"):
    base: 500 "Failed to save settings: persons: invalid birth_month for "Christine": invalid month "19711-08""
    snap: 400 "Each person needs a birth month"
  The message says the birth month is missing when one was entered -- the same false-message class conceded in ruling 2026-09-24e,
  now on the other sub-shape of the same format string. Cause: handlers_rates.go personsSaveErrorMessage matches
  `strings.Contains(msg, "invalid birth_month for")`, which wraps BOTH ParseYearMonth errors ("month is required" and
  `invalid month %q`); only the first is truthfully "needs a birth month" (the lead's validated prototype, per 2026-09-24e, keyed on
  "month is required"). It also drops the person's name that the underlying error carried.
  Browser-reachable with one keystroke: `node monthprobe.js` -> a type=month input holding "1971-08", ArrowRight + typing "19711"
  gives {"m":"19711-08","mValid":true} (Chromium accepts 5-6 digit years; the input has no max). In the real /whatif page
  (`node typo.js ... birth`, typo.log):
    snap: {"typedValue":{"value":"19711-08","valid":true},"responses":[{"status":400,...,"person_birth_month[]=19711-08"}],"visibleAlerts":["Error Each person needs a birth month"],"diskUnchanged":true}
    base: {"responses":[{"status":500}],"visibleAlerts":["Error Failed to save settings: persons: invalid birth_month for \"Christine\": invalid month \"19711-08\""]}
  Test gap that let it through: TestPersonsSaveErrorMessage's missing-birth-month case uses a synthetic error
  (`persons: invalid birth_month for "Alex": parsing time "": month out of range`) that ValidatePersons cannot produce; no test
  covers `invalid month "..."` (grep of ws5_glide_persons_test.go for "invalid month"/"month is required": only the synthetic
  "start_date: invalid month format").
  Secondary (same criterion, weaker): the start_date class (V12 "garbage", V13 "2026-13", V14 "20261-09") returns
  400 "Invalid person data: start_date: invalid month "20261-09"" -- the start date is labelled person data. Also browser-reachable
  (typo.log, use-current-month off, start date typed 20261: snap 400 "Error Invalid person data: start_date: invalid month \"20261-09\"").
  What is missing: a truthful message for the invalid-month sub-shape (e.g. naming the person and the unparseable value), a
  non-"person data" label for the start_date class, and tests using the REAL ValidatePersons/ParseYearMonth error shapes that kill
  collapsing "invalid month" into "needs a birth month".

